  "body": "Follow-up to #280. The `macos-universal (dmg)` job on the v6.45.2 release\n**built and imaged correctly** and then failed in the smoke, on its second\nline, before a single assertion ran:\n\n```\n== My Claude Code: macOS .dmg smoke ==\n  ok: image present: 4926308 bytes, 26ed2309536cefa44cc64422d583634e769d17a11d809b27646f909d690545a8\n##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\n```\n\n## Cause\n\n`smoke/macos-dmg.sh` runs under `set -euo pipefail`. Its snapshot helper lists\nboth application directories:\n\n```bash\n{\n    ls -1a /Applications 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^|/Applications/|'\n    ls -1a \"$HOME/Applications\" 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^|~/Applications/|'\n} | LC_ALL=C sort\n```\n\nA runner has no `~/Applications`. `ls` exits 2; `2>/dev/null` hides the\nmessage but not the status; `pipefail` makes the whole pipeline non-zero; and\n`snapshot > \"$before\"` under `set -e` ends the script. Reproduced locally in\nGit Bash — the old form exits 2 with `HOME` pointed at a directory with no\n`Applications`, the guarded form exits 0 with zero lines.\n\n## Two more of the same class, found while fixing it\n\n- `read_key` is `defaults read … 2>/dev/null`, which exits non-zero for an\n  absent key — and an assignment whose command substitution fails ends the\n  script under `set -e`. A missing plist key would have produced a silent exit\n  instead of the named assertion two lines below.\n- The `Authority=` extraction piped `sed` into `head -n 1`, which can close\n  the pipe early and make the substitution non-zero. `sed` already prints\n  nothing when there is no authority — which is exactly the case being\n  asserted — so `head` was never needed.\n\n## Guard\n\n`tests/contracts/test_uninstaller_parity.py::test_the_dmg_smokes_snapshot_survives_a_missing_applications_directory`\nparses the snapshot helper out of the script and asserts both listings carry\n`|| true`, plus the `read_key` guard. This is not a bug anyone re-reads a\nscript and spots; it is one you only see on a machine that lacks the\ndirectory.\n\n## Not a release\n\nNo version bump. The wheel is unaffected — v6.45.2 is published and correct,\nand this touches one shell script and one test. The shell build gets\nre-dispatched against that tag with `-f ref=main`, which is the documented\nbackfill shape.\n\n## Consequence of the failure, stated plainly\n\nBecause `release` needs `macos-dmg`, the failed job meant **no** shell assets\nwere attached to v6.45.2 — not just the `.dmg`. That is the workflow's\nexisting and deliberate semantics (`release` already needs every build leg:\n\"a failed leg means no upload happens at all\"), so it is not changed here; the\nre-dispatch attaches all eight.",
